          ],
          "content": "Orienting Yourself: The Use of Coordinates"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.867,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.083
          ],
          "content": "7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "For example, in Fig. 1.4, point S (3, – 5) is in Quadrant IV, the x-coordinate is 3 units and the y-coordinate is – 5 units. What about point Q (– 5, 3)? It is in the second quadrant, with x-coordinate – 5 and y-coordinate 3. Copy Fig. 1.4 and mark S and Q in your diagram. Mark any point P in Quadrant I and any point R in Quadrant III, and write down their coordinates."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.251,
            0.399,
            0.275
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.288,
            0.666,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "1. What is the x-coordinate of a point on the y-axis?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.311,
            0.747,
            0.331
          ],
          "content": "2. Is there a similar generalisation for a point on the x-axis?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.335,
            0.855,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "3. Does point Q (y, x) ever coincide with point P (x, y)? Justify your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.376,
            0.855,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": "4. If x ≠ y, then (x, y) ≠ (y, x); and (x, y) = (y, x) if and only if $X = y.$ Is this claim true?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.466,
            0.622,
            0.493
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 1.2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.513,
            0.886,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": "On a graph sheet, mark the x-axis and y-axis and the origin O. Mark points from (– 7, 0) to (13, 0) on the x-axis and from (0, – 15) to (0, 12) on the y-axis. (Use the scale 1 cm = 1 unit.) Using Fig. 1.5, answer the given questions."
        },
